.Dd September 18, 2026
.Dt SFX 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm sfx
.Nd simple file explorer
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Ar directory
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is a terminal file explorer.
It draws the listing of one directory on a canvas, keeps a one-line
status bar at the bottom of the screen, and reads single keystrokes in
raw mode.
.Pp
With no argument
.Nm
opens the current working directory.
With one argument it opens
.Ar directory .
With two or more arguments it prints a usage message and exits.
.Pp
.Nm
calls
.Xr chdir 2
on every navigation, so the working directory of the process always
matches the path shown in the status bar.
Relative paths given to the
.Ic \&:
prompt therefore resolve against the directory on screen.
.Pp
Every entry is rendered in
.Xr ls 1
.Fl la
format: mode string, hard link count, owner, group, size, modification
time, and name.
Hidden files, that is the files whose name starts with a dot, are listed
while the hidden-file toggle is on; the
.Ic H
key switches that toggle, and
.Pa \&.
and
.Pa ..
are always listed.
Entries are sorted with
.Pa \&.
first,
.Pa ..
second, and the rest in byte-wise alphabetical order.
.Pp
The status bar shows the first of the following that applies:
the current message, when there is one;
.Ic [tab/ntabs] k/total - /current/path ,
when more than one tab is open; or
.Ic k/total - /current/path .
.Ss Colours
The first matching rule decides the appearance of an entry.
This list is exhaustive.
.Bl -tag -width "reverse video" -compact
.It reverse video
the selection, or an entry inside the visual selection
.It dim
a non-matching entry while search dimming is active
.It bold
a directory
.It cyan
a symbolic link
.It yellow
another non-regular file: device, FIFO or socket
.It green
a regular file with any execute bit set
.It dim
a regular file whose name starts with a dot
.El
.Sh KEYS
.Ss Navigation
.Bl -tag -width "Ctrl-F, (page down)" -compact
.It Ic j , Aq down
move the selection down one entry
.It Ic k , Aq up
move the selection up one entry
.It Ic h , Aq backspace
go to the parent directory
.It Ic l , Aq enter
enter the directory, or open the file
.It Ic gg , Aq home
jump to the first entry
.It Ic G , Aq end
jump to the last entry
.It Ic Ctrl-D
move down half a page
.It Ic Ctrl-U
move up half a page
.It Ic Ctrl-F , Aq page down
move down one full page
.It Ic Ctrl-B , Aq page up
move up one full page
.It Ic Ctrl-L
re-read the terminal size and redraw
.It Ic R
force a full refresh
.It Ic H
show or hide the hidden files
.It Ic q , Q , Ctrl-C
quit
.El
.Pp
Every movement key clears search dimming and clears the current status
message, and keeps the search term itself.
.Pp
.Ic H
toggles the display of the hidden files, so the first press hides them
and the next press shows them again.
The entries
.Pa \&.
and
.Pa ..
are never hidden.
The toggle applies to the main listing and to the split-panel preview,
and it applies to every tab.
The directory is re-read, so the cursor stays on the same file name while
that name is still listed; otherwise the cursor falls back to the nearest
valid index.
The status bar then reports
.Ic hidden files shown
or
.Ic hidden files hidden .
The state at start-up comes from
.Dv SHOWHIDDEN
in
.Pa config.h .
.Ss Search
.Bl -tag -width "Ctrl-F, Page Down" -compact
.It Ic /
open the search prompt
.It Ic n
jump to the next match
.It Ic N
jump to the previous match
.El
.Pp
Matching is a case-sensitive substring test against the entry name.
On confirmation the cursor jumps to the next match and every
non-matching entry is dimmed.
.Ic n
and
.Ic N
wrap around the end of the list.
.Ss File operations
.Bl -tag -width "Ctrl-F, Page Down" -compact
.It Ic r
rename the selected entry; the prompt is pre-filled with the current name
.It Ic d
delete the selected entry, or the whole visual selection, after
confirmation
.It Ic y
copy the absolute path of the selected entry to the clipboard
.El
.Pp
Renaming is a single
.Xr rename 2
call, so it cannot move an entry across filesystems.
Deletion runs
.Ic rm -rf
on the path, so directories are removed recursively.
The entries
.Pa \&.
and
.Pa ..
can be neither renamed nor deleted.
The yanked path is written to the standard input of the
.Dv CLIPBOARD
command, which avoids all shell quoting problems.
.Ss Visual selection
.Bl -tag -width "Ctrl-F, Page Down" -compact
.It Ic V
start the visual selection, or stop it when it is already active
.It Aq Ic escape
cancel the visual selection
.It Ic j , k
extend the selected range
.It Ic d
delete every entry in the range, after confirmation
.El
.Pp
.Pa \&.
and
.Pa ..
are trimmed from both ends of the range before the count is computed, so
they are never deleted.
The selection is dropped on directory change and on tab switch.
.Ss Marks, bookmarks and tabs
.Bl -tag -width "Ctrl-F, Page Down" -compact
.It Ic ma Ns \(en Ns Ic mz
record the current entry index under that letter
.It Ic 'a Ns \(en Ns Ic 'z
move the cursor back to that index
.It Ic mA Ns \(en Ns Ic mZ
store the current directory as a persistent bookmark
.It Ic 'A Ns \(en Ns Ic 'Z
navigate to that bookmark
.It Ic t
open a new tab on the current directory
.It Ic T
open a new tab on
.Ev HOME
.It Ic gt
switch to the next tab, wrapping around
.It Ic gT
switch to the previous tab, wrapping around
.It Ic x
close the current tab
.It Ic \(ba
toggle the two-panel split view
.El
.Pp
Marks store an index, not a name, so they are meaningful only within one
directory listing, and they are lost when
.Nm
exits.
Bookmarks are written to disk as soon as they are set; see
.Sx FILES .
At most 8 tabs can exist.
Each tab keeps its own directory, cursor position, scroll offset, search
term and set of 26 marks.
The split view is drawn only when the terminal is at least 20 columns
wide.
.Sh COMMANDS
Press
.Ic \&:
to type a command in the status bar.
.Aq enter
runs it and
.Aq escape
cancels it.
The following forms are recognised, and this list is exhaustive.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ic :cd
navigate to
.Ev HOME ,
or to
.Pa /
when
.Ev HOME
is unset
.It Ic :cd Ar path
navigate to
.Ar path ;
a leading tilde is expanded to
.Ev HOME
.It Ic :sh
start an interactive shell in the current directory
.It Ic \&: Ns Ar command
run
.Ar command
through the shell
.El
.Pp
.Aq tab
completes the path being typed at the end of the line.
The first
.Aq tab
inserts the first name in the current directory that starts with what
was typed; each further
.Aq tab
replaces it with the next such name and wraps around at the end.
A completed directory carries a trailing
.Pa / ,
so a further
.Aq tab
offers the names inside it.
Candidates are sorted, and hidden names are offered only when the typed
prefix itself starts with a dot.
Any key other than
.Aq tab
ends the cycle.
.Pp
Every command other than
.Ic :sh
runs as
.Ic SHELL -i -c Qq Ar command .
The
.Fl i
flag makes the shell interactive, so it sources the rc file of the user
and expands aliases.
The listing is reloaded after the command, and the cursor stays on the
same file name when that name still exists.
.Ss Captured commands
A command whose first word is not listed in
.Va ttycmds[]
runs with its standard output and standard error captured, and with
.Pa /dev/null
on its standard input.
.Nm
stays in raw mode and does not clear the screen, so the listing stays
visible while the command runs.
.Pp
When the command produced output, that output is shown in a pane at the
bottom of the screen: a rule, then the first lines of the output.
The pane takes at most a third of the screen, and always leaves one row
for the listing.
The status line below the pane reports the command, its exit status when
that status is not 0, and how many lines did not fit.
.Aq enter
dismisses the pane; navigating to another directory dismisses it too.
Every other key leaves it on screen.
.Pp
When the command produced no output, no pane appears, and the status
line reports the command and its exit status instead.
.Pp
Control characters other than newline are replaced by spaces before the
output is shown, so a command cannot move the cursor or set attributes
on the screen.
While a captured command runs, the terminal signal keys are enabled, so
.Ic Ctrl-C
interrupts it.
.Ic Ctrl-Z
stays disabled, because stopping
.Nm
would leave the terminal in raw mode.
.Ss Full-screen commands
.Ic :sh
execs the configured shell with no arguments.
A command whose first word matches an entry of
.Va ttycmds[]
runs as an interactive shell command.
In both cases
.Nm
leaves raw mode, clears the screen and hands the terminal over for the
duration, which is what programs that draw their own screen, such as
.Xr vi 1 ,
.Xr less 1 ,
.Xr man 1
and
.Xr top 1 ,
require.
Leave the program to return to
.Nm ,
which reloads the listing.
.Pp
Only the first word is matched against
.Va ttycmds[] ,
and its directory part is ignored, so
.Ic :/usr/bin/vim notes.md
is recognised.
A program that needs the terminal but is not listed misbehaves under
capture; add it to
.Va ttycmds[]
to fix that.
.Sh CONFIGURATION
.Nm
is configured at compile time, in the suckless manner.
The
.Pa Makefile
creates
.Pa config.h
once, by copying
.Pa config.def.h ,
and never overwrites it afterwards.
Edit
.Pa config.h
and run
.Xr make 1
to apply a change.
The following settings exist, and this list is exhaustive.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Dv SHELL
the shell used for
.Ic :sh ,
for
.Ic \&:
commands, and for the clipboard pipeline
.It Dv SHOWHIDDEN
whether the hidden files are listed when
.Nm
starts: 1 lists them and 0 hides them.
The
.Ic H
key toggles the state at run time
.It Dv CLIPBOARD
the command that reads a path on standard input and copies it.
It is absent from
.Pa config.def.h ;
the compiled-in default is
.Ic xclip -selection clipboard .
Use
.Ic pbcopy
on macOS, or
.Ic wl-copy
under Wayland
.It Va openers[]
the file opener dispatch table.
Each row holds a file name suffix, or 0 for the default row that
terminates the table; the program to run with the file path as its only
argument; and a flag that is 1 to launch the program detached, for
graphical applications, or 0 to run it in the foreground
.It Va ttycmds[]
the list of commands that need the terminal, terminated by 0.
A
.Ic \&:
command whose first word matches an entry runs full-screen instead of
being captured
.El
.Pp
Build with
.Ic make USE_READLINE=1
to link against GNU readline.
This gives the
.Ic \&:
prompt and the rename prompt readline's own completion, cursor movement
within the line, and within-session history.
It replaces the built-in completion described above.
The search prompt never uses readline.
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width XDG_DATA_HOME
.It Ev HOME
the target of
.Ic :cd
with no argument and of
.Ic T ,
and the location of the bookmark file
.It Ev XDG_DATA_HOME
when set and not empty, the base directory of the bookmark file
.El
.Pp
The shell and the clipboard command are compile-time settings, so
.Ev SHELL
in the environment has no effect.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width "$HOME/.local/share/sfx/bookmarks" -compact
.It Pa $XDG_DATA_HOME/sfx/bookmarks
bookmark file, when
.Ev XDG_DATA_HOME
is set and not empty
.It Pa $HOME/.local/share/sfx/bookmarks
bookmark file, when
.Ev HOME
is set
.It Pa /tmp/sfx-bookmarks
bookmark file, otherwise
.El
.Pp
The directory is created with mode 0700 when needed.
The format is one line per bookmark: a letter, a space, then the path.
Malformed lines are skipped on load.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
.Nm
exits with status 1 when it is given more than one argument.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr ls 1 ,
.Xr rm 1 ,
.Xr sh 1 ,
.Xr stty 1
.Sh AUTHORS
.An Joel Silva Aq Mt jdbaptista@pm.me
.Sh CAVEATS
Output is byte-oriented, so a multi-byte character in a file name counts
as several columns when a line is truncated.
At most 4096 entries are read per directory, at most 256 lines and 8192
bytes are kept from the output of a captured command, and status
messages are truncated to 512 bytes.
